response:
During the 1854 Soho cholera outbreak, **John Snow** identified the immediate source.

He identified **the public water pump on Broad Street at Cambridge Street** as the source.

The observations supporting his conclusion, from the Broad Street outbreak article, were:
- he talked to local residents with help from **Reverend Henry Whitehead**;
- the **patterns of illness and death among residents in Soho** pointed to the pump;
- he noted that **nearly all the deaths had taken place within a short distance of the Broad Street pump**;
- there were **only ten deaths in houses situated decidedly nearer to another street-pump**;
- in **five** of those cases, families said they **always sent to the Broad Street pump**;
- in **three** other cases, the deceased were **children who went to school near the pump**;
- there were **61 instances** in which he was told the deceased had used Broad Street pump water.

The important limitation was that **Snow’s chemical and microscope examination of a sample of the pump water did not conclusively prove its danger**.

The parish authorities then **removed the pump handle**, disabling the pump.
